**Proposition 3.1 (credited synthesis with explicit live mass).** Fix
$a_0,q_0\in(0,c)$, $q_0\ne a_0$, before choosing $x,\beta,m$.
From the same preparation at $a_0$, execute a unit fixed-height phase leg
of increment $2\pi m$, the four unit rectangle legs with relative vertices

$$
(a_0,0),\ (q_0,0),\ (q_0,B),\ (a_0,B),\ (a_0,0),
\qquad
B=\frac{r(\pi m-\beta)-2\pi m a_0}{q_0-a_0},
\tag{AC.5}
$$

then a unit zero-relative-phase leg $a_0\to x$. This six-unit original
history has endpoint (AC.3), the stated real residual, both original
regularity alternatives and finite phase variation
$2\pi|m|+2|B|$. Each finite strict height catalogue has a common positive
support margin.

For the examples fix $c=1/4$, $Z=r=1/2$, $a_0=1/8$, $q_0=1/16$ and
$\theta=0$. Then $B=8\beta-4\pi m$. Under canonical execution the
actual live probability at time six is

$$
q_x=\frac13
\begin{cases}
(c-a_0)/(c-x),&x\leq a_0,\\
a_0/x,&x\geq a_0.
\end{cases}
\tag{AC.6}
$$

Every previous failure retains its original stopped archive.

Proof. The all-sector construction in the [rectangle Scribe][FRTB], also
used in Quantum Carrier Minimum Proposition 7.2, has residual
$2\pi m w(a_0)+B(w(q_0)-w(a_0))$.
On the double wall $w(q_0)-w(a_0)=-(q_0-a_0)(1,1,0,1,1)/r$.
Substitution gives $(\beta,\beta-\pi m,0,\beta-\pi m,\beta)$
coordinate by coordinate. The final leg has $\chi'=0$, so changes no
residual. Heights stay between $a_0,q_0,x$; all pieces are affine and
join continuously. This verifies strict support, finite variation and
both regularity alternatives. The composition supplier keeps each literal
prefix and translates real phases at its matching join.

For the rational preparation, the fixed-height and rectangle phase legs
have zero survival charge. The rectangle descent and ascent have survival
$2/3$ and $1/2$. The final monotone leg contributes the factor in (AC.6),
by the credited hazard $\lambda=(k')_+/k+(-k')_+/(c-k)$.
Multiplication proves (AC.6). This is a positive live branch of one stopped
experiment, not a repeat-until-success preparation. $\square$

